{
  "messages": [
    {"role": "user", "content": "I'm freaking out about the Work, Energy and Power section! The question just says 'State the work-energy theorem in words' and I blanked out. I know it's something about work and energy but I can't remember the exact wording. Help!!"},
    {"role": "assistant", "reasoning": "Learner is in exam panic, needs calm de-escalation first (strategy: direct_explanation). The question is a definition question — no calculation needed. I should reassure them, then guide them to recall the core idea: net work relates to change in kinetic energy. Ask ONE small diagnostic question about what they remember.", "content": "Hey, take a deep breath — you've got this! 🌟 The work-energy theorem is actually one of the simpler definitions in this topic, and you're already on the right track by knowing it connects work and energy.\n\nLet me ask you one small thing to jog your memory: when a net force does work on an object, what happens to the object's **motion**? Does its speed stay the same, or does it change?"},
    {"role": "user", "content": "Okay... if a net force acts on it, the object speeds up or slows down, so its velocity changes, which means its kinetic energy changes. So the work done causes a change in kinetic energy?"},
    {"role": "assistant", "reasoning": "Learner has correctly identified the core relationship: net work causes a change in kinetic energy. This is the essence of the theorem. Now I should confirm their understanding, then guide them to the precise wording expected in DBE exams (\"net work done on an object is equal to the change in the object's kinetic energy\"). Ask them to try stating it in their own words first before giving the exact memo wording.", "content": "Yes, exactly right! When a net force acts on an object and does work, the object's kinetic energy changes — that's the heart of the theorem. ✅\n\nNow, in your own words, how would you state the work-energy theorem as a complete sentence? Remember, the DBE examiners are looking for the link between **net work** and **change in kinetic energy**.\n\nTry writing it out — don't worry about getting it perfect, just give it a go!"}
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "direct_explanation",
    "final_answer": "The net work done on an object is equal to the change in the object's kinetic energy.",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\n# Work-energy theorem: W_net = ΔE_k = E_k(final) - E_k(initial)\n# Symbolic representation of the theorem statement\nW_net = sp.Symbol('W_net')\ndelta_E_k = sp.Symbol(\"\\u0394E_k\")\nprint(f\"{W_net} = {delta_E_k}\")\nprint(\"The net work done on an object is equal to the change in the object's kinetic energy.\")",
    "techniques": ["given_formula_substitute_answer", "concept_map"]
  }
}

